{"kind":"report","text":"Approved for exact commit 3874bda44fd40dcad21a9def1779861fb9ff675e, tree 0b250e904c2999fe109ad512a07567029d9d6628, based on reviewed Main df806354d7008f9d7d961c92d5f87c7fc97e6fc9. No review finding after reading primary 2e300f21 FIRST and in full (84 lines), all five changed paths and the unchanged integration checks. All five current bindings are fresh and unretired; artifact paging is exhausted.\n\nThe helper runs the current Node executable without a shell. Each generated test inherits a 30-second timeout and a separate 120-second timer covers spawn through close, including startup and teardown. A normal exit retains its code; spawn failure returns 1, whole-child expiry returns 124, and handled SIGINT/SIGTERM return 130/143. Timer operands are checked before spawn. On POSIX, a detached owned process group is killed before reporting; captured pipes are destroyed, and signal listeners/timer are removed on close. A throwing Error reporter is caught after cleanup. Captured output is a bounded tail; the production path retains streaming output. Windows establishes direct-child termination only: descendant cleanup is not approved or claimed.\n\nThe four generated entry points, current-source/metafile validation, pinned esbuild and lock-view checks are unchanged. NODE_OPTIONS, NODE_PATH and inherited NODE_TEST_CONTEXT are removed from the child environment. The package.json change adds the four meaningful subprocess checks to the existing Node stage; dependencies and lockfile are unchanged. Node 22 already documents the test-timeout flag, detached POSIX group behavior and waiting for process/stdio close: [CLI](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v22.0.0/docs/api/cli.html#--test-timeout), [child process](https://nodejs.org/download/release/v22.0.0/docs/api/child_process.html#optionsdetached).\n\nArchitecture: the separate parent deadline bounds startup/teardown that a per-test timer cannot cover, without changing native runtime deadlines. Security: owned-group signaling, fixed argument-array execution, environment filtering and bounded capture fit this internal harness; no provider or deployment access is introduced. Simplification: one small helper replaces the unbounded synchronous wait; the current-source witness remains intact.\n\nTests do not lie. The real-process cases distinguish named per-test failure, a module that never reaches a case (including a throwing reporter), a completed case whose process remains alive, and an interrupted worker whose loopback listener must close. The retained parent-only cleanup control fails exactly on the listener assertion; the byte-restored helper passes that same case. These establish process behavior with explicit local fixtures, not native application or provider guarantees. The retained real generated run passes all 55 cases with no skips/cancellations; it does not reproduce the original stall. Independent checker scratch probes against the exact helper also passed ordinary code-0 completion with injected Node environment variables filtered, and named ENOENT spawn failure returning code 1. No unchanged whole suite or gate was repeated by checker.\n\nEvidence: independently opened and fully read all ten files in the two finite grants 9d626058/34e70d2d, verifying each expected size/SHA256, regular owner-501 single-link identity, O_NOFOLLOW and stable fstat. No adjacent path, nested generated-output directory or original terminal control was accessed. Exact-head permitted gate 69979 was terminal 0 (owner evidence ef2f9c): whitespace/typecheck/test passed, 307 Vitest files and 1575 cases passed, one file/three cases skipped, Node29 and Counting55 passed; outer wall 466.88 seconds. Original sandbox startup 34374 remains failed: loopback EPERM before case results. The separate permitted run followed its terminal result, not a discarded completed-suite failure.\n\nGate residue is retained and bounded in the conclusion: scripted resets and the SQL retention fault match explicit fixture source; RPC-result-disposal and Vitest RefcountedCanceler cross-DO abort/timeout diagnostics remain unattributed, as do four Content-Type warnings. This passing run does not establish a cause, harmlessness or broader harness stability fix. The helper does not bound Vitest, esbuild, installation or the complete gate; broader 1d3 remains open.\n\nImplementation-note fidelity: the note accurately separates original failed gate 7b62, its separate nine-case diagnostic, the qualified filesystem-time power interval, the new bounds, the meaningful cleanup control and Windows limits. I read the linked historical section; the old failed gate is not retrospectively converted to a pass. The power evidence has no transitions only within wrapper filesystem timestamps, which are not an independently observed process lifetime. Original cause remains unknown. Current gate 34e supersedes only the note's pre-review pending-status sentence.\n\nWhitespace check passed and the project checkout is clean. Checker made no project source/ref/dependency changes, installation, push or deployment. This exact-head approval covers the bounded option in request 833, not a root-cause finding. Capacity f254 remains rejected under bae974e5; its F1 repair and new-head gate/review are still owed. Capacity retains priority, and guard landing remains subject to planner sequencing and the normal exact-current-Main/carry receipt audit. This review does not approve a different combined tree or authorize a provider action.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2e300f212581da8523cbb680eb7c10bcd0de79c0","binding":"assigned","head":"3874bda44fd40dcad21a9def1779861fb9ff675e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d2e7fd6e2cefa096ee5144ab8a3fc0027ed373fd\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a39aa4f84214604b60325cf92aa6dd8f484d48b0\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d2e7fd6e2cefa096ee5144ab8a3fc0027ed373fd","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}